V3DDriver 0.12 rev c - 4K test pack
===================================

What this is
------------
An independent RISC OS module that uses the Raspberry Pi 4's GPU (the V3D) to
draw rectangle fills and copies (window scrolling and dragging) for the desktop.
It has been hardware-tested at 1920x1080 and 1920x1200 in both 32 and 16 bits
per pixel. It has NEVER been run at 4K - nobody who has worked on it has a 4K
display - and that is what this pack is for. Thank you for helping.

Needs: Raspberry Pi 4B, Pi 400 or Compute Module 4 (the BCM2711 chip), a recent
RISC OS 5 build, and a 4K (3840x2160) display. NOT the Pi 5, NOT the Pi 3.
The module changes nothing on disk. If anything goes wrong, power-cycle.

What is in the folder
---------------------
  V3DDriver     the module
  VerifySuite   draws hundreds of fills and copies on the GPU and on the CPU and
                compares every pixel (takes a few minutes)
  GpuCheck      prints what the GPU has done so far
  TraceDump     (only if something looks wrong) shows what the driver saw

Before you start - config.txt
-----------------------------
A 4K screen needs more video memory than the default. In config.txt use
    gpu_mem=128
(or more), then reboot. For 4K at 60 Hz on a Pi 4 you also need the usual HDMI
settings (for example hdmi_enable_4kp60=1) and a cable and display that can do
it; 4K at 30 Hz works without. If your usual 4K setup already works on RISC OS,
leave it as it is and just check gpu_mem.

The test
--------
Please write down what you see at each step. Double-click files in the Filer; do
NOT type the test names into a Task Window (a Task Window swallows the drawing
the scripts test, and they will tell you so).

 1. Reboot. In the Display manager choose 3840 x 2160 and "16 million colours"
    (32 bits per pixel). Note the exact mode you chose.
 2. Double-click V3DDriver in the Filer. It loads and starts accelerating.
    Nothing is printed. Use the desktop for a minute: drag some windows about,
    scroll a long window, open a few more. Note any glitches (pieces of window in
    the wrong place, stripes, black areas, a freeze).
 3. Double-click GpuCheck. Note ALL of its output. Near the top it names the
    module version (it should say "0.12" and "rev c"). The line that matters
    most is "RESULT:" at the bottom, and whether it says the screen was ever
    declined.
 4. Double-click VerifySuite. When it asks, press 1. It takes a few minutes and
    draws on the right-hand part of the screen. It ends with either
    "ALL ... CASES IDENTICAL TO THE CPU" (good) or a list of cases that differ.
    Note the last lines, and the "Screen:" line near the top.
 5. Press the 5 option the same way: run VerifySuite again and press 5. (At 4K
    the driver cuts big rectangles into bands anyway; option 5 forces many more
    cuts, which is the harshest test.)
 6. Run GpuCheck again and note its output.
 7. Now repeat steps 3 to 6 at "65 thousand colours" (16 bits per pixel). To
    change the mode with the module loaded is itself part of the test: the
    driver has to notice the new screen. If you prefer, reboot first.

If it freezes: note the last thing you saw (and which step it was), power-cycle,
and tell us. If the screen cannot be set to 4K at all, or the desktop is blank
or garbled, say so and what config.txt contains - that tells us about memory,
not the driver.

What to send back
-----------------
  - Pi model and memory size, RISC OS version, the monitor
  - the config.txt lines you changed
  - the exact mode(s) you tested
  - the output of GpuCheck and VerifySuite (photos of the screen are fine)
  - anything that looked wrong, however small

Not being able to say "it works" is useful too: please send what you got.
